{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Methane Seep Ice: Hydrate Mounds of the Barkley Canyon Shelf",
    "date": "2026-10-11",
    "lesson": "At Barkley Canyon off Vancouver Island, methane hydrate forms crystalline white mounds directly on the seafloor where cold (~4C), high-pressure water meets upward-seeping gas. Sulfide-oxidizing mat bacteria graze the hydrate's edges, drawing energy from the chemical plume that seeps out of the ice-like mound rather than from sunlight or digested particles - true chemolithotrophy. Nearby, free-living heterotrophs scavenge the slow trickle of organic fallout that drifts down from the water above. The lesson: chemosynthesis lets life anchor to a chemical gradient alone, but only within reach of the plume - stray outside its spread and the energy income vanishes, same as a phototroph stranded below the photic zone.",
    "citation": "Ocean Networks Canada, NEPTUNE observatory - Barkley Canyon hydrate studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Barkley Canyon methane hydrate mounds, offshore Vancouver Island, ~870m depth"
  },
